NAME
       login.defs - default settings for new accounts and passwords

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/login.defs

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/login.defs holds settings read by useradd, passwd, chage and the
       other account tools. Each line is a NAME and a VALUE; # starts a
       comment. Changes apply to accounts created (or passwords set) from
       then on; existing accounts keep their values (change those with
       chage).

       The settings that matter most:

       PASS_MAX_DAYS N
              How many days a new password stays valid (99999: forever).
              Goes into the MAX field of /etc/shadow for new users.

       PASS_MIN_DAYS N
              Days before a password may be changed again.

       PASS_WARN_AGE N
              Days of warning before a password expires.

       UID_MIN, UID_MAX
              The range of UIDs for ordinary users (1000 to 60000).

       GID_MIN, GID_MAX
              The same for groups.

       SYS_UID_MIN, SYS_UID_MAX
              The range for system accounts (useradd -r).

       CREATE_HOME yes|no
              Whether useradd makes the home directory by default.

       USERGROUPS_ENAB yes|no
              Whether useradd creates a private group for each user, and
              userdel removes it.

       UMASK N
              The umask useradd uses for new home directories when HOME_MODE
              is not set (022 on RHEL 9). The umask of login sessions comes
              from the shell start-up files (/etc/profile, /etc/bashrc,
              ~/.bashrc), not from here.

       ENCRYPT_METHOD SHA512
              How new passwords are hashed.

       HOME_MODE N
              The permissions of new home directories (0700 on RHEL 9, so
              homes are private; overrides UMASK).

EXAMPLES
       Make new users' passwords expire after 90 days, with 7 days' warning:

           sed -i 's/^PASS_MAX_DAYS.*/PASS_MAX_DAYS   90/' /etc/login.defs
           sed -i 's/^PASS_WARN_AGE.*/PASS_WARN_AGE   7/' /etc/login.defs
           useradd testuser
           chage -l testuser

SEE ALSO
       useradd(8), chage(1), shadow(5), passwd(1)
